    bigIdea: 'African Americans have been an engine of the American economy from the beginning, and much of what that engine produced was taken without wages. By 1860 the people held in slavery were valued at about three billion dollars, more than all the money invested in the nation\'s railroads and factories together, and the cotton their hands grew was more than half of everything the country sold abroad. At freedom they were sent away with nothing, and the institutions they built afterward were robbed or burned. The engine is still running. The question Yahweh\'s Word puts to us is whose storehouse its yield lands in. This session measures what can honestly be measured, says plainly what cannot, and lays out what a reserve owned and governed by our own community would need: a weekly rhythm of laying by, named stewards and open books, lending without interest to the poor among us, a legal form the members own, and a heart that keeps doing good to all while it provides for its own household first.',
